(Bug #75's controls: every neighbouring shape that was already right, kept
 right by the fix. Each row here answered correctly before the store-back
 through a collection parameter existed, and must still.)

(Reading a list parameter - the direction that never needed a pointer back.)
To 'the size of' with a list called items. Return a number, items's length.

(An in-place write through a parameter: no realloc, so nothing moves. This
 is the behaviour that proves a collection parameter is a reference, not a
 copy - LANGUAGE.md's things are copies, its collections carry references.)
To 'blank the first of' with a list called items.
    Set element 1 of items to "CHANGED".

(The nearest working neighbour the manual already offered: hand the
 collection back and assign it. Still the shape a caller may write.)
To 'one more onto' with a list called items.
    append "x" to items.
    Return a list, items.

(Growth into a global from inside a function, which reads the global mirror
 rather than a parameter.)
a list called tally is ["a"].
To 'add to the tally'.
    append "x" to tally.

a list called long is ["a", "b", "c", "d", "e", "f", "g", "h", "i", "j"].
Print "read a parameter: {'the size of' of long}".

a list called two is ["a", "b"].
'blank the first of' of two.
Print "in-place write: {element 1 of two}".

a list called handed is ["a"].
a number called n is 0.
While n is less than 20,
    Set handed to 'one more onto' of handed,
    Set n to n add 1.
Print "returned and assigned: {handed's length}".

a number called t is 0.
While t is less than 20,
    'add to the tally',
    Set t to t add 1.
Print "into a global: {tally's length}".

(Top-level growth, the case that was always right because the store-back
 landed in the variable itself.)
a list called direct is ["a"].
a number called d is 0.
While d is less than 20,
    append "x" to direct,
    Set d to d add 1.
Print "at the top level: {direct's length}".

a map called counts is {"seed": 0}.
a number called c is 0.
While c is less than 20,
    Set counts's "k{c}" to 1,
    Set c to c add 1.
Print "a map at the top level: {counts's length}".

(A list a function grows through a parameter is still the caller's list
 afterwards, so the top level can keep growing it the ordinary way.)
To 'add one to' with a list called items.
    append "x" to items.

a list called shared is ["a"].
'add one to' of shared.
'add one to' of shared.
append "z" to shared.
Print "both ways: {shared's length}".
Print "last: {shared's last}".

(Reading a collection parameter the other ways round: iteration still walks
 the caller's list, and a collection past the sixth argument word - where the
 address travels on the stack rather than in a register - arrives and grows
 like any other.)
To 'show' with a list called items.
    For each item in items,
        Print "item: {item}".

To 'many' with a number called one and a number called two and a number called three and a number called four and a number called five and a list called items and a number called six.
    append "x" to items,
    Set six to one add two add three add four add five add six.

a list called pair is ["a", "b"].
'show' of pair.

a list called seventh is ["a"].
a number called s is 0.
While s is less than 20,
    'many' of 1 and 2 and 3 and 4 and 5 and seventh and 6,
    Set s to s add 1.
Print "past the sixth word: {seventh's length}".
